+# Hegel property-based testing report: `roxmltree`
+
+Crate: `roxmltree` 0.21.1 (RazrFalcon/roxmltree) — a read-only XML tree parser.
+Testing tool: `hegeltest` 0.28.2 (imported as `hegel`).
+Tests added: `tests/properties.rs` (10 live properties + 1 `#[ignore]`d reproducer).
+
+## 1. What was tested
+
+`roxmltree`'s single entry point is `Document::parse` (and `parse_with_options`),
+which produces a read-only DOM. The README makes an explicit safety promise:
+
+> This library must not panic. Any panic should be considered a critical bug and reported.
+
+Properties focus on that promise and on oracle-INDEPENDENT structural invariants
+of the resulting tree, since this is a mature, heavily-used parser where a
+differential-oracle disagreement would more likely be a bug in the oracle.
+
+Ten live properties (all in `tests/properties.rs`):
+
+1. `parse_never_panics_on_arbitrary_text` — `Document::parse` on arbitrary
+   `String` returns Ok/Err, never panics.
+2. `parse_never_panics_on_arbitrary_bytes` — same on lossy-decoded random bytes.
+3. `parse_never_panics_on_xml_shaped_input` — builds well-formed XML from a
+   generated model, then optionally applies random byte edits (near-miss
+   malformed input) to reach deep into the parser's error paths.
+4. `node_ranges_are_valid` *(oracle-independent, high value)* — for every node,
+   `range()` satisfies `start <= end <= input.len()`, both ends land on UTF-8
+   char boundaries, and a child's range is contained within its parent's range.
+5. `tree_structure_is_consistent` *(oracle-independent, high value)* — every
+   node's children report it as their parent; `first_child`/`last_child`/
+   `has_children` agree; the document element is unique; and the `descendants()`
+   preorder count equals a recursive child count.
+6. `text_content_is_preserved` — concatenated text-node content equals the
+   text placed into the model (generators avoid entity/whitespace normalization
+   so preservation is exact).
+7. `names_and_attributes_roundtrip` — element names, absence of namespace, and
+   attribute values recovered by walking model and parsed tree in lockstep.
+8. `duplicate_attributes_are_rejected` — `<e a="1" a="2"/>` yields
+   `Error::DuplicatedAttribute` (well-formedness/validation-rejection).
+9. `namespace_resolution_is_consistent` — a prefixed element with a matching
+   `xmlns:` declaration resolves to the declared URI, and
+   `lookup_namespace_uri` agrees.
+10. `entity_expansion_is_bounded` — a "billion laughs"-style nested-entity bomb
+    (generated levels × fan-out) is rejected quickly rather than expanding
+    exponentially (OOM/hang). Verifies the documented depth-10 / 255-reference
+    limits are enforced.
+
+Generators build valid XML by construction via a recursive
+`elem_gen(depth)` using `compose!`/`BoxedGenerator` (recursive `#[composite]`
+does not compile). Element/attribute names come from a name regex; text and
+attribute-value alphabets are chosen to survive parsing verbatim (excluding
+`<`, `&`, `]`, `\r`, and — for attributes — whitespace/quotes), so the
+preservation and round-trip oracles need no normalization model.
+
+## 2. Bugs found
+
+### BUG (process-aborting): deeply nested elements overflow the stack
+
+`roxmltree` parses element content by recursive descent
+(`parse_element -> parse_content -> parse_element` in `src/tokenizer.rs`) and
+enforces **no limit on element nesting depth**. Only *entity-reference* depth is
+limited (to 10, `LoopDetector` in `src/parse.rs`). A document nested only a few
+hundred elements deep overflows the stack and **aborts the process** (SIGABRT),
+violating the README's "must not panic" contract (an abort is strictly worse
+than a panic — it cannot be caught).
+
+- Empirically the overflow occurs around depth **~500** on an 8 MiB main-thread
+  stack (parses fine at 490, aborts at 500). Rust test threads use a smaller
+  (~2 MiB) stack, so it aborts even sooner under `cargo test`.
+- Minimal shape: `"<a>" * n + "</a>" * n`.
+- Reproducer: `deep_nesting_stack_overflow` in `tests/properties.rs`, marked
+  `#[ignore]` because a stack overflow aborts the whole test process and would
+  take the rest of the suite down with it. It aborts with SIGABRT when run via
+  `cargo test --test properties -- --ignored deep_nesting_stack_overflow`.
+- If `roxmltree` ever adds a nesting-depth limit, the reproducer asserts that
+  the input is rejected with `Err` instead, so it will start passing.
+
+This is oracle-independent and high-confidence: no external judgment is
+involved, and it directly contradicts a documented safety promise. Not fixed,
+per instructions.
+
+## 3. Non-bugs / things confirmed working
+
+- **Entity-expansion bombs are bounded.** The billion-laughs family is rejected
+  in microseconds; the depth-10 and 255-reference limits are enforced. No OOM
+  or hang observed across generated level/fan-out combinations.
+- **Duplicate attributes** are rejected with `Error::DuplicatedAttribute`.
+- **Node ranges** are always valid, char-boundary-aligned, and nested within
+  the parent's range.
+- **Tree structure** (parent/child agreement, unique document element, preorder
+  descendants) is consistent.
+- **Text / name / attribute-value round-trips** hold for well-formed input.
+- **Parse never panics** on arbitrary text, arbitrary bytes, or corrupted
+  XML-shaped input (aside from the deep-nesting abort above, which requires
+  hundreds of levels of nesting — never reached by the depth-bounded generators
+  in the live properties).
+
+## 4. Verification performed
+
+- **Liveness / mutation checks.** Temporarily inverting or corrupting the
+  assertion in each of the structural/round-trip properties
+  (`text_content_is_preserved`, `node_ranges_are_valid`,
+  `tree_structure_is_consistent`, `names_and_attributes_roundtrip`,
+  `duplicate_attributes_are_rejected`, `namespace_resolution_is_consistent`)
+  produced shrunk hegel counterexamples (e.g. `"<a/>"`), confirming the tests
+  can fail. Reverted afterward.
+- **Guard not vacuous.** Several properties `return` early if `Document::parse`
+  errors. Replacing that early return with a `panic!` confirmed that the
+  by-construction well-formed documents always parse, so the property bodies
+  always run.
+- **Exploratory 10x run.** Ran the whole suite at `test_cases = 1000` (10x the
+  default); all live properties passed. Confirmed the elevated count actually
+  ran via an atomic case counter (1000+ iterations observed with `--nocapture`),
+  not just wall-clock (run time rose from ~0.45s to ~3.7s). No additional bugs
+  surfaced. Reverted to default counts for the committed tests.
+- **Full suite.** `cargo test` passes: 173 + 22 + 91 + 10 unit/integration
+  tests, plus the 10 new live properties (1 ignored reproducer).
+
+## 5. Deliverables
+
+- `tests/properties.rs` — 10 hegel properties + 1 `#[ignore]`d
+  process-aborting reproducer.
+- `Cargo.toml` — `hegeltest` added as a dev-dependency (required
+  `--ignore-rust-version`: the crate pins `rust-version = 1.60`, hegeltest's
+  MSRV is 1.86; call this out if upstreaming).
+- `.gitignore` — added `.hegel`.
+- This report.
+
+Nothing in `roxmltree`'s source was modified; the bug is documented, not fixed.
